+Only the parts named in the brief were fact-checked. Other row positioning, effort claims, and the introduction's 4x to 18x claim were outside scope. Advice and ratings were not given independent truth verdicts. Advice rows above judge their factual rationale.
+
+Web search worked. Some initial fetches failed, including the English AA Haiku article and a Haiku documentation page. The Spanish-path AA article contains the English article text and was successfully fetched. The overview succeeded on retry. The obsolete Haiku prompting URL failed, but the current prompting URL succeeded. No failed fetch was treated as contradictory evidence. All undated sources were inspected on 2026-10-08. A crawl date is not a publication date.
+
+The main limitations are that most performance verdicts depend on **one independent evaluator, Artificial Analysis**, and its Haiku task-cost figures exclude the long-prompt premium. Those figures establish provisional results on its task mix, not general cost per successful task in arbitrary agent sessions. Official documentation establishes prices and defaults. The workload recommendations and Claude Code defaults above have only vendor support. Independent launch coverage corroborates release timing, but is not an independent test of every vendor assertion.
+
+Details for claims that are not HOLDS:
+
+- **H4, unsupported overall equivalence:** The draft does not say what “about level” means or which efforts are being compared. Matching low/medium/high/xhigh/max gives Haiku index scores 29/34/38/41/43 versus Luna 22/30/33/35/38. Haiku high and Luna max both score 38, so a specific equal-score comparison is supported. Replace the blanket equivalence with “same small-model class, with Haiku ahead overall at matching effort and Luna cheaper per task.” [AA effort comparison, independent, undated](https://artificialanalysis.ai/models/releases/comparisons/claude-haiku-5-5-vs-gpt-6-luna).
+
+- **H6, false generalization about tool workflows:** At max, Haiku trails on SaaS automation, 35% versus Luna's 53%, but leads on AA-Briefcase and GDPval-AA. These are also agentic workflows. Correct to “behind on SaaS automation,” without implying a general tool-use disadvantage. [AA max comparison, independent, undated](https://artificialanalysis.ai/models/comparisons/claude-haiku-5-5-vs-gpt-6-luna).
+
+- **H14, unverifiable research provenance:** The release date supports calling Haiku new. It cannot establish which research the draft's author used, or whether that research was thin. Remove the phrase “so this placement rests on thin early evidence” under the brief's shipping rule. [AA launch analysis, independent, 2026-10-07](https://artificialanalysis.ai/es/articles/claude-haiku-5-5).
+
+- **S3, stale higher-cost ordering:** Sonnet's launch measurement was $7.60 per task. Its cache reads were subsequently halved, and the current comparison lists $5.46 against Opus's $5.98. The whole phrase “about the same or more” is not wholly false because approximate parity remains defensible. Its “more” alternative should not be retained as the current measured ordering. Suggested correction: “much cheaper below max at matching effort, but near Opus's task cost at max.” [AA launch analysis, independent, 2026-09-28](https://artificialanalysis.ai/articles/claude-sonnet-5-5), [Anthropic price-cut announcement, vendor, 2026-10-07](https://www.anthropic.com/claude-haiku-5-5), [current AA comparison, independent, undated](https://artificialanalysis.ai/models/comparisons/claude-sonnet-5-5-vs-claude-opus-5-5).
+
+- **L4, unsupported general cost conclusion:** At more than 272K input tokens, the corresponding pairs favor Opus over Astra ($4/$20 versus $20/$75) and Sonnet over 6.1 Sol ($2/$10 versus $4/$15) on uncached input/output rates. Luna remains cheaper than all these larger models. Token counts, tokenizer differences, caching, and retries still determine the task bill. Correct to “at very long input lengths, Opus and Sonnet retain lower token rates than Astra and Sol respectively, while Luna has lower rates than Haiku.” These are arithmetic inferences from [Claude prices, vendor, undated](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing), [Astra, vendor, undated](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-6-astra), [Sol, vendor, undated](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-6.1-sol), and [Luna, vendor, undated](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-6-luna).
+
+Additional qualifications that matter when shipping:
+
+- **Haiku task costs are provisional.** AA explicitly says its site does not yet implement Haiku's tiered pricing. Retain the directional comparisons as evidence, but do not present the displayed dollar amounts as fully modeled costs for long sessions. The surcharge would raise affected Haiku costs, not rescue it against the cheaper Sol configuration. [AA Haiku launch analysis, independent, 2026-10-07](https://artificialanalysis.ai/es/articles/claude-haiku-5-5).
+- **The default-effort statement is correct as written.** Sonnet's API default is high, but Claude Code's default is medium. Explicit user or organization settings can override defaults. [Claude Code documentation, vendor, undated](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/model-config).
+- **“Same quality” means similar aggregate, not interchangeable strengths.** Haiku max and Sol low score 43 and 42 overall, while their knowledge-work and automation results differ substantially. [AA Haiku/Sol comparison, independent, undated](https://artificialanalysis.ai/models/comparisons/claude-haiku-5-5-vs-gpt-6-1-sol-low).
+
+Rating flags, without verdicting the ratings:
+
+- Equal execution ratings of 2 for Haiku and Luna may hide a useful difference: Haiku leads substantially on terminal work, whereas Luna leads SaaS automation. That warrants a clearer explanatory note, but the coarse rating definitions do not establish a uniquely correct replacement score. [AA max comparison, independent, undated](https://artificialanalysis.ai/models/comparisons/claude-haiku-5-5-vs-gpt-6-luna).
+- Haiku's “lowest” task-cost rating is plausible at routine effort among the listed models. It becomes misleading at max, where Sol low achieves a similar aggregate for less money. The draft's “see notes” qualification partly addresses this. [AA Haiku/Sol comparison, independent, undated](https://artificialanalysis.ai/models/comparisons/claude-haiku-5-5-vs-gpt-6-1-sol-low).
+- No evidence reviewed makes a specific numeric depth or execution rating clearly wrong under the draft's stated coarse, task-dependent definitions.
+
+Shipping-content flags:
+
+- Remove “so this placement rests on thin early evidence.” This explicitly describes the research basis. “Newly released” is ordinary release information and can remain.
+- No named research source, named study, or “this used to say” account appears in the draft. Its update date is metadata, not a research narrative.
+- No note on honesty, evaluation gaming, outages, or silent model substitution appears in the draft. Empty-output and long-thinking behavior are operational claims, not claims about any of those prohibited subjects. Their other-row factual accuracy was outside this verification scope.
+
+The draft was not edited. No subagents were used, and no files other than this report were created or modified.

